  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nUpper ridging will remain in place over most of the country, with\na very strong upper low over the Gulf of Alaska, will continue to\nbring very hot, dangerous heat through Sunday. Temperatures will\npeak tomorrow as the ridge begins to breakdown and southwest flow\nincreases. As a dry cold front approaches the region, prefrontal\ncompressional heating looks to counteract the influences of\nwildfire smoke. Tomorrow will be one of the hottest days of this\nsummer with temperatures reaching or exceeding 100. Overnight lows\nwill provide little relief as temperatures are expected to remain\nwarm. Low relative humidity and an increase in 700 mb winds\nthrough Sunday bring elevated to critical fire weather concerns \nfor the weekend. Sustained surface winds 15 to 25 mph are forecast\nto overlap with the lowest RH values, as well as ERCs above the \n90th percentile mean that any fires that start will spread rapidly\nand quickly become out of control. Issued a Red Flag Warning, \nwhich is in effect from 2 PM Saturday to 9 PM MDT Sunday.\n\nThe strong upper low over the Gulf of Alaska will move east\ntranslating to an upper trough over west-central Canada by\nTuesday. This will send a couple of cool fronts across the area,\nthe first one arriving early Sunday morning with the second\npushing through Monday morning. Wind will signal their arrival as\nonly small chances of brief showers are expected Sunday night.\nThe wind with the leftover warm air late Saturday and Sunday \ncould result in Fire Weather issues. A Fire Weather Watch remains\nin effect for this period. Windy conditions Saturday night through\nMonday will also likely need a Lake Wind Advisory.\n\nAfter Monday, the upper trough moves east and a zonal flow\nsuccumbs a bit to a Desert southwest ridge by the end of the\nforecast cycle. Expect dry conditions with temperatures warming to\nnear normal. Winds generally look light so Fire Weather issues\nshould be minimal.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nLowered min RH day 2 and 3 using a blend of HiRes models.\nIncreased winds day 2 night though day 3 using FV3 and manual\nincreases to further collaboration with neighbors.",
